rules:
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-decision-schema-must-be-structured
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All decisions must be expressed using a structured YAML decision schema
    and not as unstructured narrative text to enable machine processing and consistency.
  title: Rule Decision Schema Must Be Structured
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-adr-single-source-of-truth
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Architecture decisions must be recorded and updated only in decision records
    (ADR); all other representations must be derived from ADR to prevent inconsistencies.
  title: Rule Adr Single Source Of Truth
- id: rule-patterns-born-as-supporting
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All patterns must be created as supporting by default and never directly
    as core to ensure validation through evidence and usage.
  title: Rule Patterns Must Be Created as Supporting
- id: rule-do-not-merge-across-architectural-layers
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Patterns must not be merged or consolidated across different architectural
    layers to preserve clarity between governance, execution, contracts, and reliability.
  title: Rule Do Not Merge Across Architectural Layers
- id: rule-precedence-hierarchy
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Defines the precedence model (Global > Project > Prompt) for resolving
    conflicts between rules originating from different sources within the system.
  title: Rule Precedence Hierarchy
- id: rule-heuristic-patterns-require-signals
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All heuristic-derived patterns must include at least one linked signal
    as evidence to ensure traceability and grounding in observed behaviour.
  title: Rule Heuristic Patterns Require Signals
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-pattern-decision-lifecycle-alignment
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Ensure consistent interpretation between pattern maturity and decision
    lifecycle, preventing the conflation of knowledge evolution and governance validation.
  title: Rule Pattern and Decision Lifecycle Alignment
- id: rule-signal-promotion-criteria
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Candidate signals must satisfy mandatory criteria for recurrence, system
    impact, reusability, and stability before being promoted to a pattern.
  title: Rule Signal Promotion Criteria
- id: rule-generated-artifacts-not-authoritative
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Generated governance artifacts must summarize authoritative sources and
    must not become the authoritative source themselves to prevent state loss during
    regeneration.
  title: Rule Generated Artifacts Not Authoritative
- id: rule-workflow-execution-model-compliance
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All runtime-native workflows must conform to the CAS Workflow Execution
    Model and its separation-of-concerns principles regarding orchestration, reasoning,
    and knowledge access.
  title: Rule Workflow Execution Model Compliance
- id: rule-decision-status-drives-stage
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: The lifecycle stage of any architecture item must be derived from its decision
    status; items without 'accepted' status must not be marked as DONE.
  title: Rule Decision Status Drives Stage
- id: rule-shared-knowledge-workspace
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Projects participate in a shared Knowledge Workspace and must not create
    duplicate workspace-level governance structures when an existing workspace is
    available.
  title: Rule Shared Knowledge Workspace
- id: rule-architecture-work-authority
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Architecture Work is the authoritative source of architectural planning
    state; all generated artifacts must derive planning status from this source.
  title: Rule Architecture Work Authority
- compliance:
    enabled: true
  id: rule-aem-consistency-with-decisions
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: The Architecture Evolution Map (AEM) must remain consistent with Architecture
    Decision Records (ADR) and never contradict existing decisions.
  title: Rule AEM Consistency with Decisions
- id: rule-system-proposes-architect-decides
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All architectural decisions must be proposed by the system and explicitly
    accepted, rejected, or deferred by an architect to maintain human-in-the-loop
    control.
  title: Rule System Proposes Architect Decides
- id: rule-decisions-must-be-persisted
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: All architectural decisions must be persisted as structured decision records
    (ADR) before being reflected in AEM or other governance artifacts.
  title: Rule Decisions Must Be Persisted
- id: rule-core-patterns-must-be-foundational
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Core patterns must represent foundational architectural primitives rather
    than specific strategies or implementations to maintain long-term architectural
    stability.
  title: Rule Core Patterns Must Be Foundational
- id: rule-promotion-requires-evidence
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Pattern promotion to core must be supported by strong evidence, preferably
    from multiple sources, to ensure the core architecture remains reliable.
  title: Rule Promotion Requires Evidence
- id: rule-language-semantic-contract
  precedence: global
  scope: global
  summary: Defines the semantic contract for language used in architecture artefacts,
    distinguishing between intent, decisions, and system state.
  title: Rule Language & Semantics Contract
summary:
  global_rules: 19
  project_rules: 0
  total_rules: 19
